Edit my uploaded photo into a highly realistic, professional LinkedIn profile picture. Use this photo as the sole source for my identity. The finished portrait should look like me on the same day, wearing professional clothing and photographed in a studio. Preserving my actual face is the highest priority.


Face and expression. Preserve the original facial pixels wherever possible. Keep my head angle, gaze, expression, and the exact shape of my mouth. Retain the natural asymmetry of my face, including the height of each eyebrow, the shape and spacing of my eyes, the bridge and tip of my nose, and the contours of my cheeks, lips, chin, and jaw. Preserve my age, skin tone, undertone, hairline, hairstyle, and facial hair. Keep the small details that make me recognizable, such as freckles, moles, smile lines, and under-eye texture. If I‚Äôm wearing glasses, retain their exact frame shape, color, and fit. Limit facial adjustments to subtle exposure and color corrections.


Professional wardrobe. Dress me in a tailored charcoal blazer over a crisp, matte white shirt with the top button open. Fit the clothing to my existing build and posture. Let the shirt collar follow the angle of my neck, with a slight contact shadow where the fabric touches the skin. Give the blazer realistic lapels, fine woven texture, and natural folds at the shoulders. Keep the styling understated so the face draws attention first.


Studio lighting and background. Follow the light direction already visible on my face. Create the effect of a large, soft studio light slightly above eye level, with gentle fill on the shadow side. Preserve the soft shadow beneath my chin and the natural highlights on my forehead, nose, and cheeks. Match the lighting on the shirt and blazer to the face. Use a seamless, warm-gray studio backdrop with a subtle tonal gradient. Preserve individual strands around the hairline and clean, realistic edges around my ears and shoulders.


LinkedIn composition. Frame the portrait at eye level in a 1:1 square, from the upper chest upward. Center my face horizontally and place my eyes slightly above the midpoint. Leave enough space above my hair and around my head for a circular profile crop. Keep the face large enough for my expression to read clearly at thumbnail size. Maintain my natural head-to-shoulder proportions.


Realism and output. Use the natural perspective of an 85 mm studio portrait, with clear eyes, realistic skin texture, restrained contrast, and accurate color. Preserve fine detail already visible in the original. Avoid skin smoothing, artificial pores, reshaping, eye enlargement, teeth whitening, glossy skin, harsh sharpening, and exaggerated background blur. Keep the image free of text, logos, borders, and watermarks. Export one finished square portrait at the highest supported resolution, targeting 8192 √ó 8192 pixels where available.

endstream
